WEBVTT
Kind: captions
Language: en

00:00:00.320 --> 00:00:05.680
Sometimes what makes an exam tough is&nbsp;
not just the questions on the paper but&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:00:05.680 --> 00:00:08.720
how many other students you are competing against.&nbsp;

00:00:09.600 --> 00:00:16.000
The exam that Chinese students take to gain&nbsp;
entrance into university was last year sat&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:00:16.000 --> 00:00:22.560
by over 10 million students. That exam is&nbsp;
known as the gaokao and it's a huge deal.

00:00:23.280 --> 00:00:30.320
Students spend years preparing for the exam, often&nbsp;
with very intense study schedules and hours of&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:00:30.320 --> 00:00:38.160
extra coaching outside of school. The exam is&nbsp;
sat once per year in june and that day is taken&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:00:38.160 --> 00:00:45.760
very seriously. Traffic is diverted away from exam&nbsp;
halls and police patrol to keep the streets quiet.&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:00:46.320 --> 00:00:52.480
There are ambulances waiting outside just&nbsp;
in case of a nervous collapse. Parents will&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:00:52.480 --> 00:00:57.840
gather to pick up their children afterwards with&nbsp;
flowers to celebrate the fact that it's over.

00:00:58.480 --> 00:01:04.240
It is such high stakes because the exam&nbsp;
will give students a three-digit number&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:01:04.240 --> 00:01:09.200
which will often be the sole determinant&nbsp;
of their acceptance into university.&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:01:09.840 --> 00:01:16.560
The prestigious tier one universities are highly&nbsp;
sought after as they may offer greater employment&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:01:16.560 --> 00:01:24.480
opportunities once graduated but their acceptance&nbsp;
rate may be as low as 1 in 50 000 students.&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:01:24.480 --> 00:01:30.160
For many students doing well on the&nbsp;
gaokao may be a ticket to a better life.

00:01:30.720 --> 00:01:35.360
I have a copy of the 2020 exam here&nbsp;
and i'll go through a few of the&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:01:35.360 --> 00:01:41.200
questions just to show you what it is like.
The exam consists of compulsory sections&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:01:41.200 --> 00:01:48.560
on Chinese, math and a foreign language often&nbsp;
english and electives from physics, chemistry,&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:01:48.560 --> 00:01:55.760
biology or history, geography or politics,&nbsp;
and it takes nine hours over a couple of days.

00:01:56.320 --> 00:01:59.680
Some of the most notorious&nbsp;
questions are the essays&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:01:59.680 --> 00:02:06.480
and i've translated a few of them here. This one&nbsp;
is pretty relevant and it's all about coronavirus.&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:02:07.040 --> 00:02:13.200
They say that in the face of the outbreak they&nbsp;
have adopted prevention and control measures,&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:02:13.200 --> 00:02:19.360
that people are staying at home, venues&nbsp;
are closed and there is a safe one metre&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:02:19.360 --> 00:02:26.160
between people. They talk about the role of&nbsp;
volunteers, medical experts, workers and teachers&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:02:26.160 --> 00:02:32.400
and even journalists in fighting the virus.&nbsp;
Then the question says please synthesize&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:02:32.400 --> 00:02:38.480
the above materials and write an article&nbsp;
with the theme of distance and connection&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:02:38.480 --> 00:02:44.480
in the epidemic and they are to write an&nbsp;
essay of at least 800 words on this topic.

00:02:45.120 --> 00:02:50.800
It seems that current affairs or current&nbsp;
politics are often essay questions&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:02:50.800 --> 00:02:57.680
but so are some poetic and more unusual&nbsp;
sounding ideas. This one for example was&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:02:57.680 --> 00:03:03.120
so lovely even after passing through Google&nbsp;
Translate that I wanted to share it with you.

00:03:03.120 --> 00:03:09.120
People use their eyes to see others and the&nbsp;
world but they cannot directly see themselves as&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:03:09.120 --> 00:03:16.880
a whole. Therefore in the journey of life we need&nbsp;
to look for all kinds of mirrors, constantly draw&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:03:16.880 --> 00:03:25.120
self-portraits to examine ourselves and try to&nbsp;
answer what kind of person am I? What kind of life&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:03:25.120 --> 00:03:33.120
do I want to live? and what can I do" How to live&nbsp;
more meaningfully? and other important issues.

00:03:33.680 --> 00:03:39.920
The question asks you to write an essay as if&nbsp;
you are writing a letter to freshmen who are&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:03:39.920 --> 00:03:46.800
about to enrol in the first year of high school&nbsp;
on this theme of being able to perceive yourself.

00:03:47.360 --> 00:03:49.600
Challenging but a pretty interesting one.

00:03:50.320 --> 00:03:55.760
Since this is a physics and maths channel I'll&nbsp;
also take a look at those questions. This is&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:03:55.760 --> 00:04:02.640
one of the 2020 math papers in Chinese even&nbsp;
just from looking at the symbols and pictures&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:04:02.640 --> 00:04:09.840
you might be able to tell there are quite a few&nbsp;
word problems as well as a lot of geometry, logic,&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:04:10.400 --> 00:04:18.720
a little bit of calculus, some complex&nbsp;
numbers and some probability and statistics.

00:04:19.520 --> 00:04:22.800
I google translated question 5 to see that it says&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:04:23.440 --> 00:04:29.520
"if the circle passing the point 2,1&nbsp;
is tangent to the two coordinate axes&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:04:29.520 --> 00:04:39.280
the distance from the center of the circle&nbsp;
to the straight line 2x-y-3=0 is what?"

00:04:39.920 --> 00:04:45.360
This is my interpretation of what that looks&nbsp;
like. A circle that passes through the point 2,1&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:04:46.240 --> 00:04:52.960
and for which both the axes are tangent. We want&nbsp;
to find the distance from the center of the circle&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:04:52.960 --> 00:05:01.840
which would be at 1,1 to this straight line and&nbsp;
we'll take that to mean the shortest distance&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:05:01.840 --> 00:05:04.800
which will meet the line at a perpendicular angle.&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:05:05.520 --> 00:05:11.680
We'll want to find the equation of this line and&nbsp;
to do that we'll use the formula y-y1=m(x-x1).

00:05:18.000 --> 00:05:24.160
m or the gradient of this line will be the&nbsp;
opposite reciprocal of the line that we&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:05:24.160 --> 00:05:30.800
already know and that gradient is 2 so&nbsp;
the gradient of our line will be -1/2.

00:05:32.800 --> 00:05:38.640
Substituting ones for y1 and x1 will&nbsp;
get the equation to be y=-1/2*x+3/2.

00:05:44.960 --> 00:05:48.640
The line we already know&nbsp;
could be rewritten as y=2x-3&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:05:52.240 --> 00:05:58.160
so we can set these two equations equal&nbsp;
to each other. Doing that will give us&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:05:58.160 --> 00:06:05.360
the point where they intersect, those&nbsp;
coordinates are x equals 9/5 and y equals 3/5.

00:06:06.160 --> 00:06:11.520
Now we just want to find the distance between&nbsp;
these two known points to do that we will use&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:06:11.520 --> 00:06:21.040
the distance formula which is the square root&nbsp;
of x2 minus x1 squared plus y2 minus y1 squared.

00:06:21.040 --> 00:06:25.520
Putting our coordinates into there and&nbsp;
doing a bit of rearranging. We would&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:06:25.520 --> 00:06:31.040
find that the correct answer&nbsp;
is B, two root five over five.

00:06:31.600 --> 00:06:38.720
I think that the math involved here is reasonable&nbsp;
to expect on entrance exams in a lot of countries&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:06:38.720 --> 00:06:42.800
there are quite a few steps to this&nbsp;
one though so it's a little tricky&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:06:42.800 --> 00:06:50.080
but I think it's worth remembering that this&nbsp;
exam is not so much a certification or a&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:06:50.080 --> 00:06:56.080
test that you have learned what you've been taught&nbsp;
at school, instead this is a ranking system.&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:06:56.720 --> 00:07:01.840
It doesn't really matter that you know how to&nbsp;
do all the math steps to solve this problem&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:07:02.560 --> 00:07:08.560
and you pull it all off without making a mistake&nbsp;
it matters that you can get through the entire&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:07:08.560 --> 00:07:15.200
exam making as few mistakes as possible in&nbsp;
order to rank high among the other students.&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:07:15.840 --> 00:07:21.280
It doesn't really matter how much you know it&nbsp;
matters how much everyone else knows as well.

00:07:22.080 --> 00:07:28.400
Moving on to physics this is the 2020 Beijing&nbsp;
paper and the whole thing has been google&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:07:28.400 --> 00:07:36.240
translated. There is a bit of atomic physics&nbsp;
on the first page, thermodynamics, waves,&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:07:37.440 --> 00:07:45.680
some electromagnetism including number eight where&nbsp;
they've got a picture of a small magnetic needle&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:07:45.680 --> 00:07:53.920
hanging near a negatively charged rubber disk. The&nbsp;
disk rotates at high speed around the central axis&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:07:54.560 --> 00:08:00.160
and the small magnetic needle deflects. You're&nbsp;
then asked to determine what's going on.

00:08:00.720 --> 00:08:05.440
There's electronics, a question&nbsp;
about molecular forces,&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:08:06.160 --> 00:08:10.240
friction and one about the&nbsp;
conservation of momentum and energy.

00:08:10.800 --> 00:08:18.480
Question 14 is asking about a basketball that is&nbsp;
dropped from a height but is given a bit of spin&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:08:19.120 --> 00:08:24.880
and instead of falling straight down it&nbsp;
deviates from the vertical and moves in a curve.

00:08:25.440 --> 00:08:31.920
The second part of this physics exam seems to&nbsp;
be more about some experimental techniques.&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:08:32.640 --> 00:08:38.560
There are some classic questions about&nbsp;
experimental design and following the scientific&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:08:38.560 --> 00:08:44.320
method. The first section was multi-choice&nbsp;
but this section requires written answers.&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:08:45.040 --> 00:08:52.080
There are 20 questions in here but a lot of them&nbsp;
have multiple parts and some are pretty lengthy,&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:08:52.080 --> 00:08:57.920
for this physics paper I've shown you would get 90&nbsp;
minutes so it is still a race against the clock.

00:08:58.800 --> 00:09:03.600
In conclusion as one would&nbsp;
expect this is a difficult exam&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:09:03.600 --> 00:09:10.160
but I don't think that learning the content matter&nbsp;
is really that far out of reach for most students,&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:09:10.160 --> 00:09:16.000
in fact it is really dealing with the insane&nbsp;
competitiveness that comes with trying to get all&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:09:16.000 --> 00:09:21.840
these points without making mistakes because even&nbsp;
a tiny mistake could see your ranking plummet.

00:09:22.400 --> 00:09:29.680
Best wishes for anyone who is perhaps&nbsp;
preparing for this exam or an exam like it&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:09:29.680 --> 00:09:36.880
coming up in 2021, one day I hope you too&nbsp;
will be able to look at exams just for fun.

